<GuideLayout
  title="How to Test a New Monitor — The First 30 Minutes"
  description="A checklist for testing a new monitor while you can still return it: check the return window, warm the panel up, confirm resolution and refresh rate, then pixel faults, dark-room tests, panel quality and motion."
  heading="How to test a new monitor"
  lede="Do this before the return window closes, in this order. The sequence is not arbitrary — a few of these steps invalidate the others if you run them too early."
  updated="2026-08-27"
  slug="how-to-test-a-new-monitor"
>
  <Card surface="soft" elevation="e1" padding="lg">
    <p class="text-body-sm font-medium text-ink">In short</p>
    <p class="mt-2 text-body-sm text-body">
      Note your return deadline first, let the panel warm up for 20&ndash;30
      minutes, then confirm you are actually getting the resolution and refresh
      rate you paid for. Only then look for faults: the
      <a href="/test/sequence">twelve-fill guided run</a> for pixels, dark-room
      tests last. Keep the box until you are sure.
    </p>
  </Card>

  <h2>1. Check the return window before you check anything else</h2>
  <p>
    Find the retailer&rsquo;s returns period, work out the actual calendar date it
    ends, and write it down. Everything below is academic if the window has
    closed &mdash; a retailer return is nearly always faster and less contested
    than a manufacturer warranty claim, so the deadline is the most valuable
    piece of information you have. If the panel arrives while you are away, the
    clock is usually already running.
  </p>

  <h2>2. Let it warm up for 20&ndash;30 minutes</h2>
  <p>
    Backlight output and colour drift as the panel reaches thermal equilibrium.
    Uniformity judged in the first five minutes is not the uniformity you will
    live with, and people return panels over differences that settle out. Leave
    it on a mid-grey field and go and do something else.
  </p>

  <h2>3. Confirm the resolution and refresh rate you paid for</h2>
  <p>
    This is the single most common &ldquo;the monitor is broken&rdquo; that
    isn&rsquo;t. A cable that cannot carry the bandwidth, a port that negotiates
    down, or an operating system that quietly defaults to 60&nbsp;Hz will all make
    a good panel look bad. Check
    <a href="/test/resolution">resolution and scaling</a> and
    <a href="/test/refresh-rate">refresh rate</a> before you conclude anything
    about the hardware. If the numbers are wrong, fix the signal chain and start
    again.
  </p>

  <h2>4. Set brightness to what you will actually use</h2>
  <p>
    Not maximum. Faults look different at 400 nits than at the 120 you will sit
    at all day, and judging bleed or uniformity at full brightness will show you
    problems you would never otherwise notice. Set it where you intend to work.
  </p>

  <h2>5. Pixel faults</h2>
  <p>
    Run the <a href="/test/sequence">guided pixel test</a>: twelve full-screen
    fills in the order that makes faults easiest to see, with a tap to log each
    one. The secondaries &mdash; cyan, magenta, yellow &mdash; matter here,
    because each omits one channel and exposes a sub-pixel fault the primaries
    hide. If you find something, read
    <a href="/guides/dead-vs-stuck-pixel">dead versus stuck</a> to classify it
    correctly before you describe it to anyone.
  </p>

  <h2>6. Dark-room tests</h2>
  <p>
    These need the lights off, which is why they come late rather than first:
    <a href="/test/backlight-bleed">backlight bleed and IPS glow</a> and
    <a href="/test/uniformity">backlight uniformity</a>. Sit at your normal
    viewing distance, not six inches from the glass.
  </p>

  <h2>7. Panel quality</h2>
  <p>
    <a href="/test/banding">Gradient banding</a>,
    <a href="/test/contrast">contrast and black level</a>,
    <a href="/test/inversion">pixel inversion</a>,
    <a href="/test/sharpness">sharpness and text clarity</a> and
    <a href="/test/viewing-angle">viewing angle</a>. Sharpness is worth
    particular attention: moir&eacute; or ringing on a fine grid usually means a
    sharpness filter is switched on in the monitor&rsquo;s own menu, not that the
    panel is faulty.
  </p>

  <h2>8. Motion</h2>
  <p>
    Run <a href="/test/response-time">response time and ghosting</a> and step the
    overdrive setting through its range while you watch. Most monitors have one
    setting that is visibly better than the default and one that overshoots into
    bright inverse ghosting. A browser cannot measure grey-to-grey in
    milliseconds &mdash; this is a perceptual comparison, and it is genuinely
    useful for choosing the setting you keep.
  </p>

  <h2>9. If you found something, document it properly</h2>
  <p>
    Generate a <a href="/report">report</a> while the session is still logged.
    Photograph the fault with the monitor&rsquo;s model and serial number visible
    in the same frame &mdash; a support agent cannot dispute which unit a photo
    is of when the label is in it. Keep the packaging until the return window
    closes; some retailers will refuse or discount a return without it.
  </p>

  <h2>What is worth returning, and what is normal</h2>
  <p>
    Being honest with yourself here saves everyone time. <strong>Normal panel
    behaviour:</strong> IPS glow at the corners when viewed off-axis, a small
    amount of backlight bleed visible only on pure black in a dark room, and some
    non-uniformity across a full grey field. Every IPS panel has all three to
    some degree. Returning units until you find a perfect one is a long and
    usually unsuccessful hobby.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Worth returning:</strong> a hard-edged bright dot on black, a stuck or
    dead sub-pixel anywhere near the centre of the screen where you will look at
    it every day, banding visible in ordinary content rather than only on a
    synthetic ramp, a cluster of faults sitting close together, or bleed severe
    enough to be obvious in normal room light. Dead pixels near the extreme edge
    are a judgement call &mdash; many people stop seeing them within a week.
  </p>
  <p>
    Either way, count and classify the faults by type before you make contact. If
    a claim comes down to numbers, read
    <a href="/guides/iso-pixel-fault-classes">ISO pixel fault classes</a> and
    <a href="/guides/dead-pixel-warranty-policies">how to find the applicable
    policy</a>. The full test list is at <a href="/tests">all tests</a>.
  </p>
</GuideLayout>
